searchData={"items":[{"type":"module","title":"HvacPid","doc":"*Disclaimer: This library was implemented with the supervised help of AI.*\n\nHVAC PID is a general-purpose Elixir PID controller for discrete control\nloops. It supports direct and reverse action, independent P, I, and D\nterms, bounded output with anti-windup, a hold deadzone, and smooth\ntransitions between manual and automatic control. The caller supplies each\nsample, its elapsed time, and the actuator interface.","ref":"HvacPid.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Installation - HvacPid","doc":"Add `hvac_pid` to your dependencies in `mix.exs`:\n\n```elixir\ndef deps do\n  [\n    {:hvac_pid, \"~> 0.1.0\"}\n  ]\nend\n```\n\nThe published docs will be at  .","ref":"HvacPid.html#module-installation"},{"type":"module","title":"Quick start - HvacPid","doc":"```elixir\n{:ok, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.new(out_min: 0, out_max: 100, kp: 8, tn: 20)\n{:ok, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.set_mode(pid, :automatic)\n{:ok, command, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.step(pid, 21.0, 19.0, 0.25)\n```\n\nHere the measurement is 2 units below the setpoint, so the default\n`:direct` action gives a first command of 16. Use `direction: :reverse`\nwhen increasing the command lowers the measured value. Pass the returned\n`pid` to the next call. The `HvacPid.Controller` module docs explain the\ncontrol law and how to handle retuning, pauses, and actuator overrides.","ref":"HvacPid.html#module-quick-start"},{"type":"module","title":"Autotuning - HvacPid","doc":"`HvacPid.Autotuner` can estimate controller gains from a step response or\nfrom relay oscillations. During the experiment, the application sends\nsamples to the tuner and writes its bounded commands through the usual\nactuator interface. The module docs show how to review the result and\nresume automatic control from the output currently in effect.","ref":"HvacPid.html#module-autotuning"},{"type":"module","title":"Development - HvacPid","doc":"Run `mix test --cover` for the unit tests, simulated heating and cooling\nloops, and the 95% coverage gate. Use `mix deps.get` and `mix docs` to build\nthe module documentation locally.\n\nLicensed under [Apache-2.0](https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0).","ref":"HvacPid.html#module-development"},{"type":"module","title":"HvacPid.Autotuner","doc":"Runs a bounded plant experiment and calculates PID settings for review.\n\nChoose the experiment with `:method` in `start/2`. The default `:step`\nmethod changes the output once and observes how the plant settles. The\n`:relay` method switches between two output levels and measures the\nresulting oscillation.\n\nOnce the experiment returns a model, choose the controller form and\ntuning profile with `gains/3`. The form selects which PID terms are\nenabled. The profile selects the formula for their gains. Both choices\nhappen after identification, so you can compare several sets of gains\nfrom a single experiment.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Running an experiment - HvacPid.Autotuner","doc":"The application supplies measurements and elapsed time and writes the\nreturned commands. Keep the controller state for later and route samples\nto the tuner while the experiment is running. Send each returned command\nthrough the usual actuator writer before collecting the next sample. The\nmeasured interval should reflect the time the previous command was in effect.\nFor a step test, start near the intended operating point and choose a\nbaseline settling duration that accounts for the plant's response lag.\n\nOn completion or failure, apply the returned exit command and discard\nthe tuner state. Use `cancel/1` if an actuator write fails or an override\nchanges the applied command. Use an application watchdog to detect\nmissing samples. The tuner can check limits and timeouts when a sample\narrives. The plant can continue moving after an exit command.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#module-running-an-experiment"},{"type":"module","title":"Complete controller and autotuner example - HvacPid.Autotuner","doc":"This example runs entirely in memory. A heating loop starts in automatic\nmode at 20 °C with a 30% valve command. The simulated room has a 12-second\ntime constant and a three-second delay. The `tick` function represents\napplying a valve command, waiting one second, and reading the next\nmeasurement. The command history accounts for the delay.\n\nThe controller produces the initial valve command. The tuner takes over\nfor the step test, then returns the valve to its baseline while the\nresult is reviewed. The example accepts a PI setpoint profile, applies\nits gains, and verifies that the first automatic command matches the\noutput in effect at handoff. The following sample shows the tuned\ncontroller beginning to reduce the valve command.\n\n    iex> alias HvacPid.{Autotuner, Controller}\n    iex> setpoint = 20.0\n    iex> plant = {20.0, [30.0, 30.0, 30.0]}\n    iex> tick = fn command, {measurement, [delayed | history]} ->\n    ...>   equilibrium = 20.0 + 0.4 * (delayed - 30.0)\n    ...>   measurement = equilibrium + (measurement - equilibrium) * :math.exp(-1.0 / 12.0)\n    ...>   {measurement, history ++ [command]}\n    ...> end\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = Controller.new(out_min: 0, out_max: 100, bias: 30)\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = Controller.set_mode(pid, :automatic)\n    iex> {measurement, _history} = plant\n    iex> {:ok, baseline, pid} = Controller.step(pid, setpoint, measurement, 1.0)\n    iex> opts = [\n    ...>   out_min: 0, out_max: 100, baseline_output: baseline,\n    ...>   step_output: 50, exit_output: baseline,\n    ...>   measurement_min: 15, measurement_max: 35, min_response: 1,\n    ...>   settle_tolerance: 0.05, settle_seconds: 8,\n    ...>   timeout_seconds: 180, max_sample_interval_seconds: 2\n    ...> ]\n    iex> {:ok, command, tuner} = Autotuner.start(opts, measurement)\n    iex> result =\n    ...>   Enum.reduce_while(1..180, {tuner, command, plant}, fn _second, {tuner, command, plant} ->\n    ...>     {measurement, _history} = plant = tick.(command, plant)\n    ...>     case Autotuner.step(tuner, measurement, 1.0) do\n    ...>       {:running, next_command, next_tuner} ->\n    ...>         {:cont, {next_tuner, next_command, plant}}\n    ...>       {:ok, exit_command, model} ->\n    ...>         {:halt, {{:ok, model}, exit_command, plant}}\n    ...>       {:error, reason, exit_command} ->\n    ...>         {:halt, {{:error, reason}, exit_command, plant}}\n    ...>     end\n    ...>   end)\n    iex> {{:ok, model}, exit_command, plant} = result\n    iex> {measurement, _history} = plant = tick.(exit_command, plant)\n    iex> {:ok, gains} = Autotuner.gains(model, :pi, :chr_aperiodic_setpoint)\n    iex> {Float.round(gains[:kp], 2), Float.round(gains[:tn], 2)}\n    {3.45, 14.19}\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = Controller.configure(pid, gains)\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = Controller.set_mode(pid, :automatic)\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = Controller.track_output(pid, exit_command)\n    iex> {:ok, command, pid} = Controller.step(pid, setpoint, measurement, 1.0)\n    iex> command\n    30.0\n    iex> {measurement, _history} = tick.(command, plant)\n    iex> {:ok, command, _pid} = Controller.step(pid, setpoint, measurement, 1.0)\n    iex> Float.round(command, 2)\n    28.05\n\nOn a real actuator, apply the exit command on either terminal result\nand read back the effective output for `Controller.track_output/2`.\nTake a fresh measurement when automatic control resumes. Use the same\ncontroller direction for the experiment and subsequent control. If the\ngains are declined or the run fails, the saved controller settings are\navailable for the same handoff when the application requests it.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#module-complete-controller-and-autotuner-example"},{"type":"module","title":"Step-response experiment - HvacPid.Autotuner","doc":"Use `:step` for a self-regulating process that approaches a steady value\nat each fixed output. For example, a room may settle at one temperature\nwith a valve at 30% and a different temperature with the valve at 40%.\nThe fitted model describes the plant around those operating conditions.\n\n1. The tuner holds `:baseline_output` and watches the measurement. A\n   settled window contains at least three samples, spans at least\n   `:settle_seconds`, and has a maximum-to-minimum spread of at most\n   `:settle_tolerance`. If the spread grows too large, the window starts\n   again at the latest sample. The average of the settled window is the\n   baseline measurement.\n2. The tuner issues `:step_output` and starts recording the response.\n   The endpoint window starts when the measurement moves by at least\n   `:min_response` from the baseline. A sample that returns closer than\n   that discards the endpoint window. The same settling rule applies\n   at the endpoint. The tuner divides the change between the two\n   averaged measurements by the change in output to get the signed\n   process gain, `K`. A positive gain corresponds to direct action and\n   a negative gain to reverse action. A sign that disagrees with\n   `:direction` ends the run with `:direction_mismatch`.\n3. The tuner expresses each measurement as a fraction of the final\n   change. This makes a falling response advance from zero toward one\n   just like a rising response. It linearly interpolates the first\n   crossings of 28.3% and 63.2% at times `t₁` and `t₂` after the output\n   step. A first-order response reaches those fractions one third of\n   a time constant and one full time constant after the dead time.\n   The estimates are `T = 1.5 × (t₂ − t₁)` for the time constant and\n   `L = t₂ − T` for the dead time, both in seconds.\n4. The delay must span at least the wider of the two sample intervals\n   used for interpolation. The tuner waits until at least `L + 4T`\n   has elapsed after the step and checks every recorded measurement\n   against the fitted response. The maximum allowed error is 10% of\n   the final measurement change. A run can end with\n   `:unresolved_dynamics` when the crossings or delay cannot be\n   resolved, `:poor_fit` when the model misses the trace, or `:timeout`\n   when the plant takes too long to settle or move far enough.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#module-step-response-experiment"},{"type":"module","title":"Relay experiment - HvacPid.Autotuner","doc":"Use `:relay` when the two configured output levels can repeatedly move\nthe measurement across both sides of a target. With a target of 21 and\na `:hysteresis` half-width of 0.5, the thresholds are 20.5 and 21.5.\nIn direct action, the lower threshold selects `:high_output` and the\nupper threshold selects `:low_output`. In reverse action, the upper\nthreshold selects the high output. Between thresholds the relay holds\nits current command. At startup it chooses the command that drives the\nmeasurement toward the target, using the high output at equality.\n\nThe tuner records peaks and valleys in the timestamped measurements.\nFor reverse action it flips the sign of the measurement relative to\nthe target, so both directions use the same valley-to-valley period\ncalculation. For each cycle, the amplitude is half the difference\nbetween the intervening peak and the mean of the two valleys. The\nfirst complete cycle is discarded. The tuner then\ncollects ten cycles, requiring at least three samples in each\nhalf-cycle. Every amplitude and period in the last five cycles must\nbe within 20% of its respective median. A plant that fails to cross\nboth thresholds eventually times out.\n\nThe median period gives the estimated ultimate period `Tu`, in seconds.\nIf `d` is half the difference between the high and low output commands\nand `a` is the median measurement amplitude, the estimated ultimate\ngain is `Ku ≈ 4d / (πa)`. For example, outputs of 30% and 50% give\n`d = 10` percentage points. The estimates characterize the observed\noscillation. See [Åström and Hägglund's relay method](https://lucris.lub.lu.se/ws/files/6340936/8509157.pdf).","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#module-relay-experiment"},{"type":"module","title":"Choosing a gain profile - HvacPid.Autotuner","doc":"The step-response profiles use the fitted `K`, `T`, and `L` to calculate\na common gain scale `G = T / (abs(K) × L)`. Each profile sets `Kp` as a\nmultiple of `G` and uses either the dead time or time constant for\n`Tn` and `Td`. The controller's direction accounts for the sign of\nthe process gain. Within a given controller form, a larger `Kp` gives\na stronger proportional response. Integral strength scales with\n`Kp / Tn` and derivative strength with `Kp × Td`.\n\nA **setpoint** profile targets a change in the requested value, such\nas raising a temperature setpoint. A **disturbance** profile targets\nrecovery at a fixed setpoint after a load change, such as opening a\ndoor. The Chien–Hrones–Reswick (CHR) aperiodic profiles aim to avoid\novershoot. The 20% profiles allow overshoot in their design target\nand use larger proportional multipliers than their aperiodic\ncounterparts. Actual overshoot depends on the plant and the loop.\n\nThe entries below give `(Kp, Tn, Td)`, with omitted times set to\n`nil`. The formulas follow the WAGO *CODESYS Modules for Control\nEngineering Applications* note, pages 9–10.\n\n| Profile | Tuning objective | P | PI | PID |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `:ziegler_nichols` | Extended Ziegler–Nichols step-response rule. It gives the largest proportional multiplier for each form, tied with 20% disturbance for PID. | `(G, nil, nil)` | `(0.9G, 3.33L, nil)` | `(1.2G, 2L, 0.5L)` |\n| `:chr_aperiodic_disturbance` | Recover from a load change at a fixed setpoint with an aperiodic response. | `(0.3G, nil, nil)` | `(0.6G, 4L, nil)` | `(0.95G, 2.4L, 0.42L)` |\n| `:chr_aperiodic_setpoint` | Track a new setpoint with an aperiodic response. | `(0.3G, nil, nil)` | `(0.35G, 1.2T, nil)` | `(0.6G, T, 0.5L)` |\n| `:chr_20_disturbance` | Recover from a load change at a fixed setpoint with a 20% overshoot design target. | `(0.7G, nil, nil)` | `(0.7G, 2.3L, nil)` | `(1.2G, 2L, 0.42L)` |\n| `:chr_20_setpoint` | Track a new setpoint with a 20% overshoot design target. | `(0.7G, nil, nil)` | `(0.6G, T, nil)` | `(0.95G, 1.35T, 0.47L)` |\n\nFor the same step model with `K = 0.5`, `T = 20 s`, and `L = 5 s`,\nthe aperiodic setpoint PI rule gives `Kp = 2.8` and `Tn = 24 s`. The\naperiodic disturbance PI rule gives `Kp = 4.8` and `Tn = 20 s`.\nThat second set has both stronger proportional action and stronger\nintegral action for this plant. The two CHR objectives use identical\ngains within each overshoot category for the P-only form.\n\nThe relay result supports `:relay_pid` with the `:pid` form. Its\nrule uses the oscillation estimates directly, with `Kp = 0.25 × Ku`,\n`Tn = Tu`, and `Td = Tu / 8`. For example, `Ku = 8` and `Tu = 24 s`\ngive `Kp = 2`, `Tn = 24 s`, and `Td = 3 s`.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#module-choosing-a-gain-profile"},{"type":"type","title":"HvacPid.Autotuner.error/0","doc":"A rejected configuration, sample, experiment, or gain calculation.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#t:error/0"},{"type":"type","title":"HvacPid.Autotuner.form/0","doc":"Controller form for the generated settings.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#t:form/0"},{"type":"type","title":"HvacPid.Autotuner.method/0","doc":"Method for exciting the plant and identifying its response.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#t:method/0"},{"type":"type","title":"HvacPid.Autotuner.model/0","doc":"Measured plant characteristics returned by a successful experiment.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#t:model/0"},{"type":"type","title":"HvacPid.Autotuner.option/0","doc":"Experiment configuration. See `start/2` for required options and units.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#t:option/0"},{"type":"type","title":"HvacPid.Autotuner.profile/0","doc":"Tuning rule applied to the identified model.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#t:profile/0"},{"type":"opaque","title":"HvacPid.Autotuner.t/0","doc":"State of a running experiment. Carry it forward only on a running result.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","title":"HvacPid.Autotuner.cancel/1","doc":"Returns the exit command so the caller can apply it and discard the experiment.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#cancel/1"},{"type":"function","title":"HvacPid.Autotuner.gains/3","doc":"Calculates controller options from an identified model.\n\nStep-response profiles support `:p`, `:pi`, and `:pid`. The relay\nprofile `:relay_pid` supports `:pid`. See\n[Choosing a gain profile](#module-choosing-a-gain-profile) for the\nformulas and their design objectives. Every form enables P. Use the\nreturned `:tn` and `:td` values as-is to select I and D, including\nthe `nil` values for disabled terms.\n\n    iex> alias HvacPid.Autotuner\n    iex> model = %{process_gain: 0.5, time_constant_seconds: 20.0, dead_time_seconds: 5.0}\n    iex> Autotuner.gains(model, :pi, :chr_aperiodic_setpoint)\n    {:ok, [kp: 2.8, p_enabled: true, tn: 24.0, td: nil]}\n    iex> Autotuner.gains(model, :pi, :chr_aperiodic_disturbance)\n    {:ok, [kp: 4.8, p_enabled: true, tn: 20.0, td: nil]}\n\n    iex> model = %{method: :relay, ultimate_gain: 8.0, ultimate_period_seconds: 24.0}\n    iex> HvacPid.Autotuner.gains(model, :pid, :relay_pid)\n    {:ok, [kp: 2.0, p_enabled: true, tn: 24.0, td: 3.0]}","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#gains/3"},{"type":"function","title":"HvacPid.Autotuner.start/2","doc":"Starts a tuning experiment and returns its initial actuator command.\n\nBoth methods require `:out_min`, `:out_max`, and `:exit_output` in actuator\nunits, `:measurement_min` and `:measurement_max` in measurement units, and\npositive `:timeout_seconds` and `:max_sample_interval_seconds`.\nMeasurements must be strictly inside their limits. Commands can use\neither output bound.\n\nThe default `method: :step` also requires distinct `:baseline_output` and\n`:step_output`, positive `:settle_seconds`, and positive\n`:settle_tolerance` and `:min_response` in measurement units. The\ntolerance must be smaller than the minimum response.\n\nFor `method: :relay`, supply `:low_output` below `:high_output`, a\nmeasurement `:target`, and a positive `:hysteresis` half-width. Both\nswitching thresholds must lie strictly inside the measurement limits.\n\nThe default direction is `:direct`. The default sample budget is\n`10_000` and includes the initial measurement.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#start/2"},{"type":"function","title":"HvacPid.Autotuner.step/3","doc":"Processes a measurement and the positive elapsed time since the previous sample.\n\nApply the command in each `:running` result and retain its tuner state.\nTerminal results include the configured exit command. A measurement on\neither limit aborts the experiment. Timeout is measured from `start/2`.","ref":"HvacPid.Autotuner.html#step/3"},{"type":"module","title":"HvacPid.Controller","doc":"A general-purpose PID controller for discrete control loops. The caller\nsupplies the clock, samples, and actuator interface.\n\nKeep one state per loop and pass the state returned by each operation into\nthe next one. The controller starts disabled. Once enabled, it computes\ncommands as samples arrive through `step/4`.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Choosing a direction - HvacPid.Controller","doc":"Choose the direction based on how a larger command affects the measured\nvalue.\n\n- `:direct` is the default. The signed error is `setpoint - measurement`,\n  so it is positive when the measured value is below the setpoint. Use\n  this direction when a larger command raises the measured value, as\n  with a heater.\n- `:reverse` uses `measurement - setpoint`, so the error is positive when\n  the measured value is above the setpoint. Use this direction when a\n  larger command lowers the measured value, as with a cooler.\n\nThe direction sets the sign convention for all three PID terms.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#module-choosing-a-direction"},{"type":"module","title":"Control law - HvacPid.Controller","doc":"The controller uses the positional form of PID. The integral uses the\ncurrent error over the whole sample interval (rectangular integration).\nThe derivative uses the difference between successive samples.\n\n```text\ns       = +1 for direct action, -1 for reverse action\ne       = s * (setpoint - measurement)\nP       = Kp * e\ndelta_I = Kp * (elapsed_seconds / Tn) * e\nchange  = -s * (measurement - previous_measurement)    on measurement\nchange  = e - previous_error                          on error\nD       = Kp * Td * (change / elapsed_seconds)\nraw     = bias + tracking_adjustment + P + I + D\noutput  = clamp(raw, out_min, out_max)\n```\n\n`Kp` is in output units per measurement unit. `Tn` and `Td` are in seconds.\n`P`, `I`, `D`, the bias, and the tracking adjustment are all in output\nunits. Set `tn` to enable integral action and `td` to enable derivative\naction. Set either to `nil` to switch that term off. You can switch off the\nproportional term with `p_enabled: false` while keeping `kp` for I and D.\n\nThe derivative defaults to `:measurement`, so moving the setpoint alone\nleaves D at zero. With `derivative_on: :error`, a changing setpoint also\ncontributes to D. That can help a loop follow a ramped setpoint. A sudden\nsetpoint change can produce a large one-sample response called derivative\nkick. Both sources give the same derivative at a fixed setpoint.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#module-control-law"},{"type":"module","title":"Saturation and the deadzone - HvacPid.Controller","doc":"Before adding the new integral increment, the controller computes the raw\noutput with the previous integral. A positive increment can use the\nheadroom up to the upper bound. A negative increment can use the headroom\ndown to the lower bound. The controller discards any remainder, then clamps\nthe output. This prevents the integral from accumulating in the direction\nof saturation while allowing it to help bring the output back into range.\nThe limits apply to the combined output. For instance, I can be negative\nwith an output range of 0 to 100.\n\nA positive `deadzone` holds the previous output when the absolute error is\nat or below that radius. The integral and tracking adjustment stay fixed.\nEach held sample still updates the derivative history, so on exit the\nderivative uses the sample immediately before the exit. A zero radius\ndisables the hold. Startup and reanchoring take precedence over the hold.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#module-saturation-and-the-deadzone"},{"type":"module","title":"Startup, manual control, and retuning - HvacPid.Controller","doc":"Call `set_mode/2` with `:automatic` to start. On the first valid sample,\nthe controller emits the clamped `start_value` if one is configured.\nOtherwise it emits the clamped sum of the bias and P. I and D start at zero\nand run from the second sample onward. Setting `:disabled` clears the\ncontroller's history and immediately selects the clamped `stop_value` or,\nby default, the lower bound. Read that command with `output/1`.\n\nManual mode selects a command immediately. If an external override applies\na different command, report the effective value before returning to\nautomatic control. For example, the controller can request 35% while an\nactuator's priority logic holds it at 42%:\n\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.new(out_min: 0, out_max: 100, kp: 10)\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.set_mode(pid, {:manual, 35})\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.track_output(pid, 42)\n    iex> HvacPid.Controller.output(pid)\n    35.0\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.set_mode(pid, :automatic)\n    iex> {:ok, 42.0, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.step(pid, 21, 19, 1)\n    iex> {:ok, command, _pid} = HvacPid.Controller.step(pid, 21, 20, 1)\n    iex> command\n    32.0\n\nAfter a manual transfer, a change to the active control law, or an explicit\ncall to `track_output/2`, the next automatic sample emits the clamped\ntarget. The controller resets I and D and sets its tracking adjustment to\n`target - bias - P` using the fresh sample. The adjustment remains in the\ncontrol law on later samples. This gives proportional-only loops the same\nsmooth transfer as loops with an integral term. Tuning changes can be\ngrouped in one call to `configure/2`.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#module-startup-manual-control-and-retuning"},{"type":"module","title":"Timing, pauses, and retries - HvacPid.Controller","doc":"Pass the actual elapsed seconds between accepted samples. If you timestamp\nsamples with `System.monotonic_time(:microsecond)`, divide the difference by\n`1_000_000`. For millisecond timestamps, divide by `1_000`. For example,\n1 ms is `0.001` seconds and 250 ms is `0.25` seconds. On the first sample\nafter startup or reanchoring, any valid positive interval works because\nthat step skips the time-dependent terms.\n\nYou can skip a sample if its timestamp matches the previous one. For a\nlonger pause, decide how the actuator should behave while data is missing.\nWhen you resume, call `track_output/2` with the command currently in effect.\nThe next step uses the fresh measurement to seed D and starts a new\nintegration interval without integrating across the gap.\n\nA rejected sample leaves the old state available for retry. If you continue\nfrom that state, measure the next interval from the last accepted sample.\nSimilarly, if a write fails and you need to resume control from a command\nthe actuator actually applied, report that value before stepping again.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#module-timing-pauses-and-retries"},{"type":"module","title":"Autotuning - HvacPid.Controller","doc":"See `HvacPid.Autotuner` for a complete example that runs a step test,\ncalculates gains, and hands control back to a controller.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#module-autotuning"},{"type":"module","title":"A PI example - HvacPid.Controller","doc":"With a gain of 8, each unit of signed error contributes 8 units to the\nproportional term. The default direct direction gives a positive error\nof 2 when the measurement is 19 and the setpoint is 21.\n\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.new(out_min: 0, out_max: 100, kp: 8, tn: 20)\n    iex> HvacPid.Controller.output(pid)\n    0.0\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.set_mode(pid, :automatic)\n    iex> {:ok, command, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.step(pid, 21, 19, 0.25)\n    iex> command\n    16.0\n    iex> {:ok, command, _pid} = HvacPid.Controller.step(pid, 21, 19, 0.25)\n    iex> command\n    16.2\n\nWith the same tuning in reverse, a measurement of 23 gives the same\npositive error and first command.\n\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.new(out_min: 0, out_max: 100, kp: 8, tn: 20, direction: :reverse)\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.set_mode(pid, :automatic)\n    iex> {:ok, command, _pid} = HvacPid.Controller.step(pid, 21, 23, 0.25)\n    iex> command\n    16.0","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#module-a-pi-example"},{"type":"type","title":"HvacPid.Controller.error/0","doc":"The reason an operation was rejected.\n\nOption errors identify a missing bound, an unknown or duplicate key, or an\ninvalid value. `:invalid_options` means the options were given in a form\nother than a keyword list. `:invalid_output_limits` means the lower bound\nexceeded the upper bound.\n\nAn invalid input error identifies the affected argument. A\n`:numeric_overflow` means a calculation exceeded the available floating\npoint range even though the supplied inputs were valid.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#t:error/0"},{"type":"type","title":"HvacPid.Controller.mode/0","doc":"A controller mode or a manual command in output units.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#t:mode/0"},{"type":"type","title":"HvacPid.Controller.option/0","doc":"One configuration setting. See `new/1` for defaults and units.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#t:option/0"},{"type":"opaque","title":"HvacPid.Controller.t/0","doc":"Opaque controller state returned by the controller operations.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","title":"HvacPid.Controller.configure/2","doc":"Applies a batch of settings to an existing controller.\n\nAny options omitted from the batch keep their current values. Both bounds\nare checked together, so a range can be moved in one operation. An invalid\nbatch returns an error and leaves the caller's state available.\n\nDuring automatic control, a change to gain, term selection, derivative\nsource, direction, bias, or bounds reanchors the next sample to the current\ncommand or pending tracked value. Further changes before that sample retain\nthe target within the new bounds. Changing only the deadzone or the start\nand stop values keeps the current control history. A controller still\nawaiting its startup sample uses the latest settings for startup.\n\nBounds clamp the current command immediately. Widening the range later\nkeeps the clamped manual command. In disabled mode the command is\nrecalculated from the new stop value and bounds.\n\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.new(out_min: 0, out_max: 100)\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.configure(pid, out_min: 120, out_max: 200, kp: 3)\n    iex> HvacPid.Controller.output(pid)\n    120.0","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#configure/2"},{"type":"function","title":"HvacPid.Controller.new/1","doc":"Creates a disabled controller with the specified settings.\n\n| Option | Default | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `:out_min`, `:out_max` | Required | Lower and upper output bounds. Equal bounds give a fixed command. |\n| `:kp` | `1.0` | Shared, nonnegative gain in output units per measurement unit. |\n| `:p_enabled` | `true` | Whether to include the proportional term. |\n| `:tn` | `nil` | Positive integral time in seconds, or `nil` to disable I. |\n| `:td` | `nil` | Positive derivative time in seconds, or `nil` to disable D. |\n| `:derivative_on` | `:measurement` | Use changes in measurement, or include setpoint changes with `:error`. |\n| `:bias` | `0.0` | Constant contribution in output units. |\n| `:deadzone` | `0.0` | Nonnegative hold radius in measurement units. Zero disables the hold. |\n| `:direction` | `:direct` | Signed error is setpoint minus measurement in `:direct` and measurement minus setpoint in `:reverse`. |\n| `:start_value` | `nil` | Optional output for the first automatic sample after enabling. |\n| `:stop_value` | `nil` | Optional output for disabled mode. The lower bound is used when omitted. |\n\nNumeric options accept integers and floats that can be represented as\nfinite floats. Each key can appear once. See `t:error/0` for errors.\n\n    iex> {:ok, pid} = HvacPid.Controller.new(out_min: -20, out_max: 80, stop_value: 0)\n    iex> HvacPid.Controller.output(pid)\n    0.0\n    iex> HvacPid.Controller.new(out_max: 100)\n    {:error, {:missing_option, :out_min}}","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#new/1"},{"type":"function","title":"HvacPid.Controller.output/1","doc":"Returns the most recent output command in output units.\n\nManual and disabled mode changes update the command immediately. This\nvalue represents the controller's command. Use `track_output/2` to report\nthe effective actuator output when an override applies.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#output/1"},{"type":"function","title":"HvacPid.Controller.set_mode/2","doc":"Selects disabled, automatic, or manual operation.\n\nDisabled and manual commands take effect immediately. Automatic operation\nwaits for a fresh sample. When coming from manual mode it anchors to the\nlast manual command or to the most recent tracked value. Reapplying the\ncurrent mode is idempotent. Two manual requests that clamp to the same\ncommand count as the same mode.\n\nReturns `{:error, :invalid_mode}` for an unsupported mode, or\n`{:error, {:invalid_input, :manual_output}}` for an invalid manual value.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#set_mode/2"},{"type":"function","title":"HvacPid.Controller.step/4","doc":"Advances the controller using a setpoint, measurement, and elapsed seconds.\n\nThe setpoint and measurement must share units. All three arguments must\nbe finite numbers, and elapsed seconds must be strictly positive. Fractional\nintervals are used as supplied. Manual and disabled steps validate their\ninputs and return the current command. Manual steps also remember the\nsample, while transfer to automatic control seeds history from a fresh one.\n\nReturns `{:ok, command, state}` on success, or `{:error, reason}` for\ninvalid input or numeric overflow. See `t:error/0`.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#step/4"},{"type":"function","title":"HvacPid.Controller.track_output/2","doc":"Reports the output currently applied at the actuator.\n\nIn automatic mode the latest report becomes the target for the next\nsample, including when reported between enabling and the startup sample.\nIn manual mode it supplies the later transfer target while the manual\ncommand stays in effect. Selecting a different manual command clears that\ntarget. Disabled mode keeps its stop command and starts afresh on enable.\n\nTracking clamps the reported value to the bounds and leaves `output/1`\nreporting the current command until the next automatic sample. Returns\n`{:error, {:invalid_input, :applied_output}}` for an invalid value.","ref":"HvacPid.Controller.html#track_output/2"}],"proglang":"elixir","content_type":"text/markdown","producer":{"name":"ex_doc","version":"0.40.4"}}